Analysis:
In `CheckChainRevocation`, the revocation check is performed on the certificate chain:
```cpp
  size_t chain_length = sk_X509_num(chain);
  if (chain_length > kMaxChainLength || chain_length == 0) {
    return 0;
  }
  // Loop to < chain_length - 1 because the last cert is the trust anchor/root
  // which cannot be revoked
  for (size_t i = 0; i < chain_length - 1; i++) {
    X509* cert = sk_X509_value(chain, i);
    X509* issuer = sk_X509_value(chain, i + 1);
    int ret = CheckCertRevocation(provider, cert, issuer);
    if (ret != 1) {
      return ret;
    }
  }
  return 1;
```
If a peer presents a certificate chain of length exactly 1 (`chain_length == 1`), `chain_length - 1` evaluates to 0. Consequently, the loop condition `i < 0` is false, and the loop is completely skipped. As a result, the function returns 1 (revocation check passes), completely bypassing CRL revocation verification for single-certificate chains.

**Conceptual Exploit Payload:**
An attacker initiates a TLS connection and presents a revoked single certificate that is directly trusted by the client/server (e.g., self-signed or directly in the trust store). During the TLS handshake, OpenSSL builds a verified chain of length 1. The `CustomVerificationFunction` is triggered, calling `CheckChainRevocation` which returns 1 without checking the CRL because the loop is skipped. The connection is successfully established despite the certificate being revoked.

Snippet:
static int CheckChainRevocation(
    X509_STORE_CTX* ctx, grpc_core::experimental::CrlProvider* provider) {
#if OPENSSL_VERSION_NUMBER >= 0x10100000
  STACK_OF(X509)* chain = X509_STORE_CTX_get0_chain(ctx);
#else
  STACK_OF(X509)* chain = X509_STORE_CTX_get_chain(ctx);
#endif
  if (chain == nullptr) {
    return 0;
  }
  // BoringSSL returns a size_t (unsigned), while OpenSSL returns an int
  // (signed). In OpenSSL, a -1 can indicate a problem. By forcing it into a
  // size_t, a -1 return will result in the chain_length being a very large
  // number, so it will still fail this check because that very large number
  // will be >= kMaxChainLength
  size_t chain_length = sk_X509_num(chain);
  if (chain_length > kMaxChainLength || chain_length == 0) {
    return 0;
  }
  // Loop to < chain_length - 1 because the last cert is the trust anchor/root
  // which cannot be revoked
  for (size_t i = 0; i < chain_length - 1; i++) {
    X509* cert = sk_X509_value(chain, i);
    X509* issuer = sk_X509_value(chain, i + 1);
    int ret = CheckCertRevocation(provider, cert, issuer);
    if (ret != 1) {
      return ret;
    }
  }
  return 1;
}

Analysis:
In `looks_like_ip_address`, gRPC attempts to quickly check if a peer name looks like an IP address. If it returns 1, CN (Common Name) matching is bypassed and only SAN (Subject Alternative Name) checks are performed. However, the heuristic has a major logic flaw:
```cpp
static int looks_like_ip_address(absl::string_view name) {
  size_t dot_count = 0;
  size_t num_size = 0;
  for (size_t i = 0; i < name.size(); ++i) {
    if (name[i] == ':') {
      // IPv6 Address in hexadecimal form, : is not allowed in DNS names.
      return 1;
    }
    if (name[i] >= '0' && name[i] <= '9') {
      if (num_size > 3) return 0;
      num_size++;
    } else if (name[i] == '.') {
      if (dot_count > 3 || num_size == 0) return 0;
      dot_count++;
      num_size = 0;
    } else {
      return 0;
    }
  }
  if (dot_count < 3 || num_size == 0) return 0;
  return 1;
}
```
A host string like `1.2.3.4` returns 1. But a hostname like `123.456.789.123` also returns 1 because the dot count is 3, each label consists only of numbers <= 3 digits, but the value is outside the valid IPv4 octet range (0-255).
If a server certificate's Subject Common Name matches `123.456.789.123`, a connection to `123.456.789.123` (which is a valid hostname, not an IP address) will see `looks_like_ip_address` return 1. Consequently, `tsi_ssl_peer_matches_name` will bypass CN matching:
```cpp
  // If there's no SAN, try the CN, but only if its not like an IP Address
  if (san_count == 0 && cn_property != nullptr && !like_ip) {
    if (does_entry_match_name(absl::string_view(cn_property->value.data,
                                                cn_property->value.length),
                              name)) {
      return 1;
    }
  }
```
This forces SAN checks, and since there is no SAN matching `123.456.789.123`, the handshake will fail or bypass verification inconsistently, or allow certificate matching bypasses depending on wildcards. Specifically, it enables bypassing Common Name matching when connecting to hostnames that resemble invalid IPs, leading to denial of service or spoofing under specific name configurations.

**Conceptual Exploit Payload:**
An attacker sets up a service with a valid hostname like `999.888.777.666`. The user tries to connect to `http://999.888.777.666:8080/`. If the server has a valid certificate issued for `999.888.777.666` in the Common Name, the gRPC TLS client verification callback parses the peer name `999.888.777.666`, mistakenly identifies it as an IP address, and completely skips checking the Common Name. Since there are no SAN fields in the certificate matching the peer name (or if they are absent), the validation fails unnecessarily (DoS). Conversely, if the client has a wildcard SAN, a malicious target could exploit this heuristic mismatch.

Snippet:
static int looks_like_ip_address(absl::string_view name) {
  size_t dot_count = 0;
  size_t num_size = 0;
  for (size_t i = 0; i < name.size(); ++i) {
    if (name[i] == ':') {
      // IPv6 Address in hexadecimal form, : is not allowed in DNS names.
      return 1;
    }
    if (name[i] >= '0' && name[i] <= '9') {
      if (num_size > 3) return 0;
      num_size++;
    } else if (name[i] == '.') {
      if (dot_count > 3 || num_size == 0) return 0;
      dot_count++;
      num_size = 0;
    } else {
      return 0;
    }
  }
  if (dot_count < 3 || num_size == 0) return 0;
  return 1;
}

Analysis:
In `GetCrlFromProvider`, the provider calls `provider->GetCrl(cert_impl)`. The `DirectoryReloaderCrlProvider` and `StaticCrlProvider` implement `GetCrl` by looking up the CRL exclusively using `certificate_info.Issuer()`, which is the Subject Issuer DN string of the certificate.
However, authority certificates can share the same Issuer Distinguished Name (DN) while possessing different public keys (e.g., during certificate authority key rotation or roll-overs). A proper CRL retrieval MUST query both the `Issuer` and the `AuthorityKeyIdentifier` (AKID) to ensure the CRL matches the correct signing key of the issuer.
Because the provider map (`crls_` in `grpc_tls_crl_provider.cc`) is a `flat_hash_map<std::string, std::shared_ptr<Crl>>` keyed solely on the issuer name, CRLs from different CAs with identical issuer names collide. In `DirectoryReloaderCrlProvider::Update()`, the map is populated by iterating through directory files. If multiple CRLs have the same Issuer DN, the last one loaded will overwrite the previous ones in the map.
During signature validation in `VerifyCrlSignature(crl, issuer)`, if the wrong CRL is returned due to a name collision, signature validation fails:
```cpp
  valid = grpc_core::VerifyCrlSignature(crl, issuer);
  if (!valid) {
    gpr_log(GPR_DEBUG, "Crl signature check failed.");
  }
```
This causes `CheckCertRevocation` to fail and return `0`, rejecting the peer handshake. This enables a low-privileged local/remote entity to trigger a Denial of Service (DoS) by inducing CRL name collisions, causing all handshakes of certificates issued by affected CAs to fail.

**Conceptual Exploit Payload:**
An attacker drops a malicious or conflicting CRL file into the CRL directory monitored by `DirectoryReloaderCrlProvider`. The CRL specifies a matching Issuer DN to a legitimate CA but is signed by a different key (or is simply a different CRL from another CA with the same name). When the reloader refreshes, the map `crls_` is overwritten with the conflicting CRL. During the TLS handshake with a legitimate client, `GetCrl` retrieves the conflicting CRL. `VerifyCrlSignature` fails validation because the signatures do not match the expected CA key, rejecting the connection and causing a Denial of Service.

Snippet:
absl::Status DirectoryReloaderCrlProvider::Update() {
  absl::flat_hash_map<std::string, std::shared_ptr<Crl>> new_crls;
  std::vector<std::string> files_with_errors;
  absl::Status status = crl_directory_->ForEach([&](absl::string_view file) {
    std::string file_path = absl::StrCat(crl_directory_->Name(), "/", file);
    // Build a map of new_crls to update to. If all files successful, do a
    // full swap of the map. Otherwise update in place.
    absl::StatusOr<std::shared_ptr<Crl>> crl = ReadCrlFromFile(file_path);
    if (!crl.ok()) {
      files_with_errors.push_back(
          absl::StrCat(file_path, ": ", crl.status().ToString()));
      return;
    }
    // Now we have a good CRL to update in our map.
    // It's not safe to say crl->Issuer() on the LHS and std::move(crl) on the
    // RHS, because C++ does not guarantee which of those will be executed
    // first.
    std::string issuer((*crl)->Issuer());
    new_crls[std::move(issuer)] = std::move(*crl);
  });
  if (!status.ok()) {
    return status;
  }
  MutexLock lock(&mu_);
  if (!files_with_errors.empty()) {
    // Need to make sure CRLs we read successfully into new_crls are still
    // in-place updated in crls_.
    for (auto& kv : new_crls) {
      std::shared_ptr<Crl>& crl = kv.second;
      // It's not safe to say crl->Issuer() on the LHS and std::move(crl) on
      // the RHS, because C++ does not guarantee which of those will be
      // executed first.
      std::string issuer(crl->Issuer());
      crls_[std::move(issuer)] = std::move(crl);
    }
    return absl::UnknownError(absl::StrCat(
        "Errors reading the following files in the CRL directory: [",
        absl::StrJoin(files_with_errors, "; "), "]"));
  } else {
    crls_ = std::move(new_crls);
  }
  return absl::OkStatus();
}
